\exercise{Exercise 1 -- Arithmetic, geometric, and harmonic means}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A distribution takes values $x_i=10,20,30$ with counts $n_i=2,3,5$. Calculate the arithmetic mean.
\item Revenue changes by $+10\%$, $-5\%$, and $+20\%$ in three successive years. Calculate the average annual growth rate using the geometric mean of growth factors.
\item A vehicle travels 120 km at 60 km/h and then 120 km at 90 km/h. Calculate the average speed and explain why the harmonic mean is used.
\end{enumerate}
\exercise{Exercise 2 -- Measures of central tendency for grouped data}
Use the following willingness-to-pay distribution:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrrrr}
\toprule
Price class (euros) & $[10,14[$ & $[14,18[$ & $[18,26[$ & $[26,30[$ & $[30,35[$ & $[35,40[$ & $[40,45[$ & $[45,50[$ & $[50,55[$\\
\midrule
Count $n_i$ & 4&12&32&32&60&35&25&5&5\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Identify the modal class, taking unequal class widths into account.
\item Calculate the median by interpolation.
\item Calculate $Q_1$ and $Q_3$ by interpolation.
\item Calculate the approximate arithmetic mean using class midpoints.
\item Interpret the modal class, median, mean, and quartiles.
\end{enumerate}
\exercise{Exercise 3 -- Price affordable to 60\% of customers}
Find the maximum price such that about 60\% of customers can afford the product. Use the cumulative curve or linear interpolation in the relevant class and explain the reasoning using cumulative frequencies.

