\solution{Detailed solution -- Exercise 1}
\[
\bar x=\frac{2(10)+3(20)+5(30)}{10}=23.
\]
For growth:
\[
1+g=\sqrt[3]{1.10\times0.95\times1.20}=1.078365,
\]
so $g\approx\boxed{7.84\%}$.
For equal distances:
\[
H=\frac{2}{1/60+1/90}=\boxed{72\text{ km/h}}.
\]
\solution{Detailed solution -- Exercise 2}
The count densities are $1,3,4,8,12,7,5,1,1$, so the modal class is \boxed{$[30,35[$}.

Median: rank $105$ lies in $[30,35[$:
\[
Me=30+5\frac{105-80}{60}=\boxed{32.08}.
\]
Quartiles:
\[
Q_1=26+4\frac{52.5-48}{32}=\boxed{26.56},
\]
\[
Q_3=35+5\frac{157.5-140}{35}=\boxed{37.50}.
\]
Using midpoints,
\[
\sum n_ic_i=6665,\qquad \bar x=6665/210=\boxed{31.74\text{ euros}}.
\]
The modal class has the greatest density; the median divides the observations into two equal halves; the mean is the average based on class midpoints; $Q_1$ and $Q_3$ delimit the central 50\% of the distribution.
\solution{Detailed solution -- Exercise 3}
If 60\% can pay, 40\% must be below the price. The target cumulative count is
\[
0.40(210)=84.
\]
Since the cumulative count is 80 at 30 euros and 140 at 35 euros, interpolate in $[30,35[$:
\[
p=30+5\frac{84-80}{60}=\boxed{30.33\text{ euros}}.
\]

